    "problem": "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\nx\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation.",
